Chapter 1: Why Do We Need Two Pointers?

Suppose the array is sorted.

[1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10]

Question:

Find two numbers whose sum is 12.

Brute Force

Check every pair.

1 + 2

1 + 4

1 + 6

...

8 + 10

Nested loops.

for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
    {
        if(nums[i] + nums[j] == target)
            return {i, j};
    }
}

Time Complexity

O(n²)

Can we do better?

Yes.

Chapter 2: The Main Idea

Instead of checking every pair,

start from both ends.

L                 R
↓                 ↓
1 2 4 6 8 10

Target = 12

Current sum

1 + 10 = 11

Too small.

What should we do?

Move Left.

1 is too small.

Now

  L             R
  ↓             ↓
1 2 4 6 8 10

Sum

2 + 10 = 12

Found it.

Done.

Only one pass.

Time

O(n)
Why Does This Work?

Because the array is sorted.

If

Left + Right

is

too small,

moving Right leftwards would only make the sum smaller.

So the only hope is to move Left to a bigger number.

Likewise,

if the sum is too big,

move Right left.

That's the intuition.

Chapter 3: The Basic Template
int left = 0;
int right = nums.size() - 1;

while(left < right)
{
    int sum = nums[left] + nums[right];

    if(sum == target)
    {
        // Found answer
    }
    else if(sum < target)
    {
        left++;
    }
    else
    {
        right--;
    }
}

You'll see this exact structure repeatedly.

